JVM property Default Optional values and definition <code>jvector.ash.queryProjection</code> <code>auto</code> <code>auto</code>: INT8 where an integer dot-product backend exists, then optimized FP16, falling back to FP32. <code>fp32</code>: FP32. <code>fp16</code>: require optimized FP16 with FP32 accumulation/output. <code>int8</code>: require INT8 projection with integer accumulation and FP32 output. Explicit unsupported precisions fail. Default for models without query options set in code. <code>jvector.ash.singleKernel</code> <code>auto</code> <code>auto</code>, <code>scalar</code>, <code>simd</code>; single-vector ASH scoring kernels, subject to provider/format support. <code>jvector.ash.blockKernel</code> <code>auto</code> <code>auto</code>, <code>scalar</code>, <code>simd</code>; blocked/fused ASH scoring kernels, subject to provider/format support. <code>jvector.ash.queryBatchSize</code> <code>8</code> Positive integer below <code>Integer.MAX_VALUE</code>; maximum opportunistic batch for model-owned synchronous query preparation; <code>1</code> disables combining. Separate from the queued batch limit below. Default for models without query options set in code. <code>jvector.ash.gatheredScoring</code> <code>false</code> Experimental. <code>true</code> scores a node's neighbors from in-memory 2- or 4-bit codes as one gathered block, as fused ASH scores its stored neighborhoods (one landmark, backend support required). It lowers single-query latency, but with every core busy it has measured 1–6% lower throughput. <p>Single-vector scoring (separated codes, streaming indexes and fused upper layers) depends on the bit width. 1-bit codes use a SIMD masked add over their sign bits. 2- and 4-bit codes use a projection dot that decodes codes in registers: native Highway kernels, or with Panama a contiguous-query kernel on JDK 25+ and a query-gather kernel on earlier JDKs. The other widths use the native nibble-LUT dot; with Panama they are decoded in scalar Java, so prefer 1, 2 or 4 bits there. Fused graphs score base-layer neighborhoods with whole-block kernels at every width.</p> <p>To choose these per model in code, call <code>setQueryOptions</code> before the model projects its first query, for example right after opening an index. Later calls throw <code>IllegalStateException</code>, because the prepared projection is fixed for the model's lifetime.</p> <pre><code>var ash = ((FusedASH) index.getFeatures().get(FeatureId.FUSED_ASH)).getASH();
ash.setQueryOptions(ASHQueryProjection.AUTO, 1);  // precision, maximum batch
</code></pre> <p>See Choosing ASH query-projection precision for processor guidance and FP16/INT8 requirements. Under <code>symmetric-all</code>, query precision affects application queries, not insertion search. Under mixed or asymmetric construction, it can affect insertion too.</p>
